# Email voice and ending — LOCKED

## Voice

Every email draft to opposing counsel, legal staff, court personnel,
mediators, and other litigation participants must sound:

- smooth and natural;
- professional, respectful, and polite;
- concise and direct without sounding mechanical;
- firm when necessary, without hostility or performative legal language.

Use straightforward questions for scheduling and logistics. Prefer natural
phrasing such as:

> Are there any dates coming up that would work well for you and the team?

Avoid stiff filler such as "I remain interested in exploring," "if your client
is amenable," or "before the parties incur additional litigation costs" when a
plain sentence or question does the job. Use legal preservation language only
when it serves a concrete purpose.

Address recipients by the correct title and surname when known. Verify the
honorific instead of defaulting to "Mr." For the current FedEx team, use
"Ms. Kray," "Ms. Bow," and "Ms. Wilson" as applicable.

Tone does not change substance. Do not add concessions, waive positions,
soften deadlines, alter settlement authority, or change a requested number in
the name of sounding polite. Settlement-number drafts remain subject to the
settlement guard.

## Ending

Every covering email, reply to counsel, and outgoing message closes with
exactly these two lines after the valediction (usually "Thank you,"):

```
Brendan Ngwa Nforbi
Plaintiff, In Propria Persona
```

Do not add a phone number, email address, "In Pro Per," "Regards," or
"Brendan Ngehsi Newanforbi" to the close. Pleadings and signature blocks
on filed papers are unchanged by this rule unless the user asks.
